<ContentLayout title="Aurora OS — From-Scratch Rust Operating System | Aurora" description="Learn about Aurora OS, its goals, development status and current installation availability." eyebrow="ECOSYSTEM / OPERATING SYSTEM" heading="Aurora OS: an operating system built from scratch" lead="Aurora OS is an independent operating-system project being built in Rust. Its goal is to combine reliability, privacy and security by default with meaningful choice over how people use their computers. It is still in development.">
  <section class="section shell content-columns">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">DEVELOPMENT STATUS</p><h2>In development</h2><p class="content-copy">Aurora OS is not yet available as a verified public installation release. The source repository is private, and this website does not claim that individual features are ready for everyday use.</p><p class="eyebrow">CAN I INSTALL IT?</p><h3>Not yet</h3><p class="content-copy">There is no official public installer or tested installation guide today. We will publish one here when it is safe to use.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="/en/downloads/">Check downloads <span aria-hidden="true">↘</span></a></div>
    <div><p class="eyebrow">WHAT AURORA AIMS TO PROVIDE</p><ul class="content-list"><li>Privacy and security as default design requirements.</li><li>Reliable behavior and protection for user data.</li><li>A system that can be adapted to different people and needs.</li><li>Optional components rather than one mandatory desktop experience.</li><li>Clear explanations of permissions and system activity.</li></ul><p class="evidence-note">These are project goals, not a claim that every capability is already implemented.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="section shell article-content">
    <h2>An operating system for different people</h2>
    <p class="content-copy">The goal is for Aurora to be approachable for someone who simply wants a computer that works, while still giving experienced users control over their environment. A person should not need to understand kernels, services or security engineering to use the everyday features safely.</p>
    <p class="content-copy">Aurora is intended to be configurable: people should be able to choose the applications and interface they need, without being forced to run a full graphical desktop if they do not want one. Essential security and recovery protections must remain in place regardless of the chosen configuration.</p>
    <h2>Technical direction</h2>
    <p class="content-copy">The project's stated direction is a capability-based, microkernel-oriented system. The aim is to make resource authority explicit, constrain privileged responsibilities and organize suitable functionality behind defined service boundaries. These are design goals, not claims that particular mechanisms are already implemented or verified.</p>
    <div class="principle-grid">
      <article class="principle-card"><span>01 / KERNEL</span><h3>Constrained privileged core</h3><p>Define which responsibilities must remain privileged and which can be delegated to services. The actual boundary requires public design and implementation evidence.</p></article>
      <article class="principle-card"><span>02 / AUTHORITY</span><h3>Explicit capabilities</h3><p>Specify how authority is represented, granted, transferred, restricted and revoked. Concrete capability semantics remain to be documented and verified.</p></article>
      <article class="principle-card"><span>03 / SERVICES</span><h3>Defined service contracts</h3><p>Document ownership, IPC contracts, lifecycle and failure behavior before claiming isolation or fault containment.</p></article>
      <article class="principle-card"><span>04 / VALIDATION</span><h3>Evidence-led progress</h3><p>Publish source references, test environments, acceptance criteria and limitations for each subsystem when they can be reviewed publicly.</p></article>
    </div>
    <h2>What is publicly established today?</h2>
    <p class="content-copy">The public information establishes the project's language and architectural direction, but it does not establish a supported computer list, a verified installation process, a usable everyday desktop, storage guarantees, driver coverage or an installable system. Those details will be published when concrete specifications, implementation and repeatable test results are available.</p>
    <h2>Repository access</h2>
    <p class="content-copy">The Aurora OS repository is private. The public <a href="https://github.com/Pokejoueur/aurora-website" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Aurora Website repository</a> contains the source for this website, not the OS implementation. The project's future source-availability policy and any timeline for changing repository visibility have not been published.</p>
